            {
              "id": "preamble.archive",
              "name": "PreambleArchive",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "7z or RAR archive header in the preamble"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The 128-byte preamble is the first thing any non-DICOM program reads, and PS3.10 leaves it free for application use, so it can make one file two formats at once. Archive tools that identify files by their first bytes open it as an archive."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Extract the bytes at the reported offset (`radguard map` shows the file's layout) and identify them. Conforming software never stores data where readers do not look, so the content is either a writer bug or deliberately hidden.",
                "markdown": "The 128-byte preamble is the first thing any non-DICOM program reads, and PS3.10 leaves it free for application use, so it can make one file two formats at once. Archive tools that identify files by their first bytes open it as an archive.\n\n**What to do:** Extract the bytes at the reported offset (`radguard map` shows the file's layout) and identify them. Conforming software never stores data where readers do not look, so the content is either a writer bug or deliberately hidden.\n\n**References:**\n- <https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2019-11687>"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Aahil-Riyaz/radguard/blob/main/docs/rules.md#preamblearchive",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "hidden-data",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-436"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5"
              },
              "relationships": [
                {
                  "target": {
                    "id": "436",
