            {
              "id": "preamble.mz-header",
              "name": "PreambleMzHeader",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS/PE header in the preamble"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The preamble starts with an MZ header, but e_lfanew does not point at a PE signature, so the file is not runnable as it stands. No imaging software writes MZ into the preamble: this is usually a truncated or staged PE/DICOM polyglot."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Quarantine the file and keep it away from anything that could run or extract the embedded content. Extract the payload at the reported offset for malware analysis, find out how the file entered the archive, and check other files from the same source. Re-saving the file in a viewer is not a fix: it may keep the payload, and it destroys evidence.",
                "markdown": "The preamble starts with an MZ header, but e_lfanew does not point at a PE signature, so the file is not runnable as it stands. No imaging software writes MZ into the preamble: this is usually a truncated or staged PE/DICOM polyglot.\n\n**What to do:** Quarantine the file and keep it away from anything that could run or extract the embedded content. Extract the payload at the reported offset for malware analysis, find out how the file entered the archive, and check other files from the same source. Re-saving the file in a viewer is not a fix: it may keep the payload, and it destroys evidence.\n\n**References:**\n- <https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2019-11687>"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Aahil-Riyaz/radguard/blob/main/docs/rules.md#preamblemz-header",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
