            {
              "id": "preamble.nonstandard",
              "name": "PreambleNonstandard",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unrecognised non-zero preamble"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The 128-byte preamble is the first thing any non-DICOM program reads, and PS3.10 leaves it free for application use, but nearly all software writes zeros or a TIFF header. Unrecognised content is not evidence of an attack on its own; it is worth identifying because it is what other programs see first."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Extract the bytes at the reported offset (`radguard map` shows the file's layout) and identify them. Conforming software never stores data where readers do not look, so the content is either a writer bug or deliberately hidden.",
                "markdown": "The 128-byte preamble is the first thing any non-DICOM program reads, and PS3.10 leaves it free for application use, but nearly all software writes zeros or a TIFF header. Unrecognised content is not evidence of an attack on its own; it is worth identifying because it is what other programs see first.\n\n**What to do:** Extract the bytes at the reported offset (`radguard map` shows the file's layout) and identify them. Conforming software never stores data where readers do not look, so the content is either a writer bug or deliberately hidden.\n\n**References:**\n- <https://dicom.nema.org/medical/dicom/current/output/chtml/part10/chapter_7.html>"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Aahil-Riyaz/radguard/blob/main/docs/rules.md#preamblenonstandard",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "hidden-data"
                ],
                "precision": "medium",
                "security-severity": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "preamble.ole",
              "name": "PreambleOle",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "OLE compound file header in the preamble"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The 128-byte preamble is the first thing any non-DICOM program reads, and PS3.10 leaves it free for application use, so it can make one file two formats at once. The format of MSI installers and legacy Office documents, both of which can carry code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Quarantine the file and keep it away from anything that could run or extract the embedded content. Extract the payload at the reported offset for malware analysis, find out how the file entered the archive, and check other files from the same source. Re-saving the file in a viewer is not a fix: it may keep the payload, and it destroys evidence.",
                "markdown": "The 128-byte preamble is the first thing any non-DICOM program reads, and PS3.10 leaves it free for application use, so it can make one file two formats at once. The format of MSI installers and legacy Office documents, both of which can carry code.\n\n**What to do:** Quarantine the file and keep it away from anything that could run or extract the embedded content. Extract the payload at the reported offset for malware analysis, find out how the file entered the archive, and check other files from the same source. Re-saving the file in a viewer is not a fix: it may keep the payload, and it destroys evidence.\n\n**References:**\n- <https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2019-11687>"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Aahil-Riyaz/radguard/blob/main/docs/rules.md#preambleole",
