            {
              "id": "preamble.pdf",
              "name": "PreamblePdf",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PDF header in the preamble"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The 128-byte preamble is the first thing any non-DICOM program reads, and PS3.10 leaves it free for application use, so it can make one file two formats at once. PDF readers accept a header anywhere in the first kilobyte, so the file also opens as a document."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Extract the bytes at the reported offset (`radguard map` shows the file's layout) and identify them. Conforming software never stores data where readers do not look, so the content is either a writer bug or deliberately hidden.",
                "markdown": "The 128-byte preamble is the first thing any non-DICOM program reads, and PS3.10 leaves it free for application use, so it can make one file two formats at once. PDF readers accept a header anywhere in the first kilobyte, so the file also opens as a document.\n\n**What to do:** Extract the bytes at the reported offset (`radguard map` shows the file's layout) and identify them. Conforming software never stores data where readers do not look, so the content is either a writer bug or deliberately hidden.\n\n**References:**\n- <https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2019-11687>"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Aahil-Riyaz/radguard/blob/main/docs/rules.md#preamblepdf",
